CREW: April the 3rd, 1995. The survivor is Barbara Rona, interviewer Iris Lasky. Los Angeles, USA. It will be in English.

INT: My name is Iris Lasky. I am conducting an interview with Barbara Rona. We are in the city of Culver City, state of California, country of the United States of America, interview being done for the Survivors of the Shoah Foundation, and today's date is April 3rd, 1995.

Barbara, would you please start by giving us your full name and spelling it, please?

BR: OK. My name is Barbara Rona, B-A-R-B-A-R-A, last name Rona, R-O-N-A.

INT: Was this the name given to you at birth?

BR: No. Rona, first of all, is my married name. Today's my husband's birthday, by the way-- late husband, unfortunately. And I was born Rozsi Spinner. My father's name was Joseph Spinner. My mother's name was Rose or Rozsi [? Alexander. ?]

And my mother died at my birth. And I was a premature baby. And I was so tiny, that they named me the equivalent of a doll in Hungarian, which was Babu. And I-- I grew up with that name, and I found it when I was already a grownup person, kind of silly. And in the United States, when we became citizens, I chose the name Barbara because that was the closest in my mind for that nickname, Babu.

INT: Were you known by any other nicknames or names?

BR: No, no, no. That's it. That was plenty for me. [LAUGHS]

INT: When were you born?

BR: I was born October 26, 1923.

INT: And the city?

BR: The city, Békéscsaba, Hungary. It's a small town of about 50,000 people. It was a rural town, terribly antisemitic. And all around the town, there were farms. So they were very simple people.

However, there was a-- a quite a cultural life in-- in that small town. And we had a opera, for instance, in the season. And we had a museum, art museum, and all the other things. Not too-- too many things were, however, available for me as in my teen years, as I'm going to go into it a little bit.

INT: Describe your family, cousins, aunts, grandparents.

BR: So as I said, my-- I told you my maternal-- my-- my-- my mother's name. And my grandmother on my mother's side, her name was Ilona Rosenbaum. And that was her maiden name. She-- she was married to my grandfather, whose name was [? Maurice ?] [? Alexander. ?]

And my father was Joseph Spinner. And his parents were [? Helen ?] Haber, H-A-B-E-R, and [? Simon ?] Spinner. And I was his-- an only child. And I had no brothers and sisters, but my father had about seven or eight siblings, and they were all married, and I had numerous aunts and uncles and countless cousins.

Some of them I didn't even know. And they, unfortunately, except the three sisters, my father's brother's three daughters, they survived. And two of them lives in-- live in Israel. They, all of them, perished. So we counted at one point, about 75 members of my family, only on my father's side.

And on my mother's side, I had a second cousin who was with me and my aunt during the Holocaust. Her name was [? Lily ?] [? Weinblum. ?] And she is still alive. She lives in Hungary, in a different town.

And so I-- if I may, I will tell you a little bit about my early childhood, which was very, very difficult, growing up without a mother. And my grandmother raised me. My grandmother was a widow. Her husband died in-- in the influenza epidemic after the First World War. And they-- my aunt, Irene, who was the most important person, almost, in my life, she was my mother's sister. And she was only 14 years older than I was.

So my grandmother and she looked after me. And when I-- I have a picture of me when I was a very little-- two, three years old. Would you care to see that, or later?

INT: Later.

BR: And anyhow, when I was about five years old, and it was time-- time to go to school, Jewish children were not allowed in public schools in the elementary level. And so I had to go to school to a Jewish elementary school, where I'm sure the educational system, even then, was better than in another public school. But the point is that I had to pass by a regular public school for Gentile children.

And I had to walk in the middle of the street, because the kids were throwing rocks at me. And it was absolutely the most incredible feeling. You know, I was-- I was totally ignorant. I didn't understand what did I do. I'm only five years old. I didn't even know those kids, and they threw rocks at me, because I was a Jewish kid.

And I went to school for four years in this elementary school. And we had a-- a typical Jewish life, in a way. I didn't see much of my father, whom I absolutely adored. I thought that the sun was rising and set on him.

He was a textile salesman, and he was traveling a lot. And so he usually came home only on Friday afternoons, and he stayed for-- until Sunday, and then he was on the road again. So needless to say, those Shabbats were the highlight of my life as a child growing up. And that lasted for a while.

But then the winds of war began to blow. And the Hungarians were, I think, outdoing the upcoming and uprising Hitler. And they came up with anti-Jewish laws. And there was only a certain percentage of Jewish people who were allowed to work, to-- to have a-- a job.

And unfortunately, my father was one of those who were dismissed. And so things were extremely difficult, very difficult. My grandmother found, you know, she was doing some sewing, and my aunt learned to be a milliner, you know, to make hats. And so somehow we struggled through. But it was very, very, very lean years.

INT: What years would this have been?

BR: Well-- well, these laws-- I was in-- in around 13 years old, so it must have been in the early to mid '30s. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And--

INT: Tell--

BR: Yes, I'm sorry?

INT: Tell me a little bit more about the religious life. You said Shabbats were very important.

BR: Oh, yes. Shabbat because we always had a Shabbat dinner, and my father and I went to temple. We had two-- two synagogues in-- in my town. By the way, I think it was just around 2,000 Jewish people in-- in our town. The town, as I said, was 50,000. So was 2,000. It was a good size.

We had three synagogues, actually. But the third one was in a-- like a storefront for Sephardic. There were a few Sephardic people. So we had a Conservative and a very Orthodox temple. And I-- the Conservative temple-- we didn't have names like we have names here for temples. But it was a very-- really a very lovely temple.

The women sat upstairs, separately. And there was a choir. My husband-to-be, which I didn't know it at that time, was singing in that choir. And so did my brother-in-law. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

Yeah, Shabbat, we-- we managed. Somehow we eked out enough so we had a nice Shabbat meal. And it-- it-- it was-- you know, it was fun. And we get together.

We-- that was-- we didn't-- I didn't have any transportation, of course. Nobody had a car in those days. But even if we had, we wouldn't have driven a car or-- or-- or rode in any public conveyance.

So we went visiting family. And there was no telephone either, so we just dropped in, or somebody dropped in in our house. And it was just expected that that's what's going on on Shabbat afternoons. And then, you know, came Sunday, and back to the salt mines, so to say.

INT: Can you describe what your home looked like?

BR: It was very very simple, a very simple home. The kitchen was the-- the center of activity. It wasn't a very large kitchen. We had to move when my father lost his job. And up till-- up till that time, we had a very lovely apartment. But after that, we couldn't afford it, and so we moved into a very small place.

And we had one-- the kitchen, as I said. And we didn't have running water. So we had-- I think there were four or five dwellings in that unit. And so on-- in the-- in the yard, they had four or five outhouses. And my-- I remember, my aunt wallpapered ours with the pictures from a fashion magazine, so it shouldn't look so awful.

Anyhow, so we had to heat water to-- to-- to take a bath in-- in washtubs. And then once a week, we went to a-- a bath in a bath house. So-- but you know, it's a funny thing, that you can get used to anything. I thought that was perfectly natural [LAUGHS] thing to do.

INT: Did you have any activities or hobbies that you enjoyed?

BR: No, no. I did not. I really didn't. It was-- you know, life was different when I was a child. It seems to be such a long time ago. And it's-- interestingly enough, I somehow, because of a painful childhood, I blocked out most anything and everything.

And I-- I don't know if this belongs to this interview or not, but I have a recurring dream for the last 20 years or so. I have two recurring dreams. One is that I am pushing a buggy, a baby buggy or a doll buggy, I don't know, with a baby in it.

And it's in mud, a field of mud. And this buggy gets stuck in the-- in the mud. And-- and I am frantically trying to push it. And the next thing I know, there is no baby in there.

That's-- I talked about-- and the other dream is that I'm trying to find my home. Even here, in this-- in this area, and there is obstacles. They just pop up, tall buildings that I cannot go through. I can't find my way home. I turn here, on-- on this way or that way, and there's always something in front of me.

So I talked to a friend who is a-- a professor of psychology. Actually, I spoke to his students at one time. And he-- and he said that it's a very simple explanation. That baby is you, and you are searching for your lost childhood.

So the reason I brought this up, because you really don't have to be a psychologist to figure it out, that that's what I didn't have. I didn't have-- this horrendous war, this horrendous thing that happened to us ruined-- took away my childhood, my-- my-- my young womanhood when I-- when I was a teenager. I had maybe one or two years in my life that was really lovely. Not totally free of problems, but it was lovely. And that was that.

And so one of the reasons that we-- my husband and I never spoke to our children about this, because we wanted to raise perfectly normal, healthy, mentally healthy children. And so I wanted them to have lovely memories of their childhood, not like their mom.

INT: Can you describe the years that were so lovely?

BR: Yeah. I was 16 years old. I had-- I had-- as-- as poor as I was, I had one dress for the summer, and one dress for the winter. [LAUGHS] And I had lots of friends. And there was one young man especially. And we fell in love. And his name was [? Carey, ?] [? Carey ?] [NON-ENGLISH].

And he-- I-- I really loved him with all of my heart, just as a young first love can be. And he, unfortunately, never came home. But we became engaged to get married, against my father's [LAUGHS] wishes. He wasn't very happy about a 16, 17-year-old girl getting engaged.

But 16, 17, I was mentally and emotionally mature enough, like today a-- a young woman in her 20s would be. Because life made me grow up in a jiffy. And so he-- he and I spent lots of beautiful, you know, lovely, innocent love. And that-- that gives me a little warmth every time I think of it.

I-- for many years, I was able to look at that girl or that child that I was completely detached, like an out-of-body experience. I looked at her as a-- and-- and-- and felt nothing but great pity for that child, for that young person. And then eventually, after-- after the pain started to gnaw at me, it-- it-- it-- I-- it was me again. So I-- I wasn't divorcing myself from that person. I don't know if I make myself clear.

So that's the way life went. And after the elementary schools, I was talking about my schooling, I went to an all-girls school. It was a-- a-- there were two public schools for girls and boys. They were-- boys and girls were separate.

And so I went to this school for four years. And I remember there was a-- a girl who-- who lived across the street from us. And her father was a school principal. And we went to the same school, from the same street. And she walked on one side of the street, and I walked on the other side of the street, because she was not allowed to associate with me.

And then we were 11, 12 years old. So you know, it is very sad, really. Because children were taught from little kids, childhood on, that the Jews were somebody to despise, that the Jews were not to be trusted, that Jews were Jesus killers, or we-- we killed their gods. And-- and we take the blood of-- of children to-- to make matzah for Passover. And I grew up with this.

So anyhow, I went to this school for four years. And then things became very sticky, and my father thought that I should better learn some kind of a trade. And I went for the next-- for the four years.

All together, it takes 12 years to go to school there also, but I went to a school where academics were taught, but office skills were emphasized. So I learned to type and bookkeeping and so on. I never used it. And that was the end of my schooling in-- in that country. Because it was interrupted, unfortunately.

INT: You mentioned that you did go to this school for office skills.

BR: Yes.

INT: Did you have other goals, that you looked towards the future at one time?

BR: No, no. The future was just some totally unknown fog, that I have absolutely no idea. I-- I didn't know. I was hoping that I would get married. But-- and-- and be a-- you know, in Europe, girls were raised to be good wives and-- and-- and mothers and not really raised for a profession, unless you-- you went to the university, which I never had a chance to.

INT: How did you discuss, or what did you discuss about the antisemitic feelings in your home?

BR: I don't really remember. I really don't remember. I don't even remember being a-- a question. I think it was just a way of life, unfortunately. And you know, when I was in-- in my early teens, in this all-girls school, the teachers themselves were antisemitic, and they humiliated you in every way possible.

I recall an incident when I-- I was never good in math. I'm still not. [LAUGHS] I recall, he called me up to-- to solve a problem at the chalkboard, and I got stuck. I couldn't figure out that equation.

And-- and so he said to the-- in front of the-- the whole class, said, well, well, you don't know how to do that. I thought Jews were very good with numbers. And it was a-- it was just an accepted fact that it is-- is pummeled into you from birth that you are less than other people, which is, you know, it is-- it's terrible, what you can do to a-- a-- a child's psyche. But I grew up. And I hope I am a relatively sane person today. [LAUGHS]

INT: Do you recall friendships that you did have with other young girls?

BR: Only Jewish, Jewish girls. And Jewish girls and boys, I had a slew of friends, slew of friends.

INT: What activities would you do together?

BR: Oh, we rode bicycles. And-- and the-- the young man, who was my beloved, they had a-- in the outskirts, they had a-- a-- like a-- not a farm, but like a-- a rural little house, and they grew grapes. There was lots of grapes. And we used to go there, a whole bunch of us, on the bicycles, on handlebars.

And we spent like picnics there. And we had a-- it wasn't a river. It really, it was a canal. But it looked like a-- a small river in our town.

And we walked around the banks of that river, and, you know, looking at the trees. You had willow-- willow-- weeping willow trees on the-- either side of that river and looked at it. And I loved nature, even then.

INT: When did you become first aware of the rise of Hitler and the-- and the Nazi power?

BR: Well, most likely from the newspapers and the radio. There was no television, of course. And it was a-- a-- we had great deal of apprehension, a great deal of apprehension.

First of all, because the Hungarians embraced that philosophy with all open arms. They were absolutely just thrilled. And of course, you know, that's-- the history shows that Hitler's success was because of his-- he had to have a scapegoat of the-- the terrible economic conditions in Germany. And so he-- he convinced first Germany, and then later on all the-- the whole Europe that the Jews are the ones who are responsible for the tough times.

And that's-- and we knew, of course. The papers were full of it. But I don't really think that anybody in their wildest imagination would have expected anything what happened to us would happen.

After all, we were assimilated Hungarians, so to say, even though that my father was born in a place where it changed hands from Czechoslovakia to Hungary and back and forth. And I don't even-- I don't think that we were actually citizens. Because I know that he could never vote.

And so-- but we didn't speak Yiddish in the house. And it was only Hungarian. And so it never occurred to anybody. So you know, hard knocks might come, but what will happen, no one-- no one had any inclination. And believe me, not until the very, very last moment, nobody would have ever, ever suspected such horror.

INT: How would you get information, and what would you hear about what was going on?

BR: Well, you know, not until the-- actually, the very last days did we know what awaits us. We never knew anything. And if the rabbis or Jewish organizations knew anything about what Hitler was doing, and because the concentrations were-- camps were popping up by then. But nobody-- no one knew about it in Hungary, no one.

And so, you know, he-- he started first in-- in Austria, in 1938. 1938, I believe, and so little by little, yeah. Because the war started in '39. So the first country was Austria that he occupied.

And-- and they-- they were-- people were lining the streets and cheering, cheering him on. And no one was very upset about Hitler, because everybody was promised two chickens in every pot.

INT: Describe your first memories of signs of danger.

BR: Signs of danger were when the men, able-bodied young men-- that was in 1942, in Hungary, all-- all were taken in a labor camp, from-- from the age of 18 up to 50. Everybody was taken away.

First they were in the country, and so we could correspond. And my father was one of them and my husband, because he was eight years older than-- than I was. And so he was one of them. I-- I-- I-- he was a friend only, because we lived in the same building.

But that's-- that's how we knew that something is not right. Because they were not taken into a-- a-- as soldiers. They were not given uniforms.

But they were attached to-- to Hungarian Army units. And the only weapon they had was a shovel. And so they had to dig ditches, and then later on, terrible, terrible things.

INT: Do you remember the day that they were-- that they left?

BR: In 1942, what day? It-- it-- it must have been in-- in the summer of '42. Whether it was July or August, I am not sure. And then things went downhill after that. Things were getting worse and worse. Of course we didn't-- you know, it was very difficult.

But before the men were taken away, my father-- because Jews were not allowed to own any businesses-- my father and a partner, a friend of his, under an assumed name, and a person-- a Gentile person's name, they opened a textile store in Budapest. And it was going just very well. Nobody was-- that was in '42.

And so the business went on almost for a-- several months, close to a year. Maybe it was '41 when they opened that store. And so I went to visit him. And I had a grand old time with him. As I told you before, I just absolutely adored him. I was a very well-developed girl even then, and he was a very young-looking man. And he got a kick out of taking me places, so they thought that this guy has a nice young girlfriend.

Anyhow, the business went well until one day, when my maternal grandmother died, and my father and I went to the funeral. And during the time when we were away for the few days in 19-- end of 1942, it was discovered that-- that his business was running under a false name. They used to call those people straw men. Don't know if there was such a-- a-- an expression in English or not. Because they-- they just got a certain percentage of the proceeds, but the business was run by my father and his partner.

Anyhow, my-- my father's partner was arrested for lying. And consequently, he was put to jail. And ironically, he survived the war in the jail.

And my father, when we came back, had to go to the-- to this labor camp and never came back. And so I will talk about him later, what happened to him. Because that's the next-- next year of my-- of my story. And so if my grandmother didn't die, I would have had my dad, maybe.

INT: What was your parting to your father like when he left?

BR: I knew it. When I last saw him, I knew it, that this is the last time I see him. I had that-- that inner feeling. He put me on the train, from Budapest, to go home to Békéscsaba. And-- and I looked at him as long as I could see him. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] I didn't see him again. [PAUSES FOR 8 SECONDS] [DRINKS] I'm sorry. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

And I went back and life went on. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And I-- you know, I kept sending letters but never got one back, to him. And for a while, I-- I-- I wrote letters almost every day to my young man.

And I got a few postcards from him until all these people were taken to Russia and to be-- he was at the-- my husband was at the-- and I think he also-- at the Siege of Leningrad, you know, which lasted in that horrendous war for about nine months. [PAUSES FOR 7 SECONDS]

So anyhow, we somehow managed to live. And then I have a feeling that my aunt-- oh, I didn't tell you that-- that when I was 12 years old, my father married my Irene.

INT: In your home?

BR: Yes. But I was going to tell you that when I was 12 years old, my father married my Aunt Irene. And I'm going to just refer to her as Irene. And she was also one of those people who if I said that this was black, it was black because I said so. And I could do no wrong in her book.

And so she was-- she became my stepmother. And so we-- we-- we went-- we survived the war together. But it was very important for us, both of us, to-- we all-- I think we both saved each other's lives, actually.

So when I went back home, then, you know, we continued to live. And I don't know how-- Irene must have found out something, but they didn't tell it to me, that they know something was not quite right. And so she thought that they were looking for some field workers for a-- a baronial farm or something, like a plantation of sorts, and they needed young people, young girls, you know, boys and girls to work on the field.

And so Irene thought that if I went to that, nothing would happen to me, because I'm-- I'm doing farm work, which is the necessary. So I was there for about a week or 10 days with-- a whole bunch of us went. And-- and we were-- it was a backbreaking job, I remember.

We had to thin carrots. And, you know, bending over all day long in that beating down sun was not much fun. But the time came, anyhow, that I had to return to my home. And during the 10 days when I was there, the Jews were concentrated in a-- a certain area of the town as-- as a ghetto. And their houses were all marked with a large star and a-- the word Jew in Hungarian written on their house.

Hitler came to-- to Hungary in March 19, 1944. And-- and this was going on, what I'm talking to you, about end of May. So I had to leave this farm job, and we all had to go back. And we went to my house. And my-- my own place was designated as one of the ghettos, so we-- we remained there for the few remaining days.

Now, there was all radios, bicycles, were confiscated. So we didn't really know what's happening. But you know how rumor factories are working. So from mouth to mouth, it was-- we knew that we are-- something drastic is going to happen.

The one day-- well, every day, they came up with some kind of new orders. You have to do this, you have to do that. And we had to wear the-- I think there were two hours of the day allowed when we could go out of the house to-- if-- if we could find some food to buy, if we had anything to buy it with, that is.

And two hours were allowed for that. And we were not allowed to leave the house without wearing the yellow star, which also said Jew on it. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] It is an undescribable-- or indescribable feeling when you accept this, that you-- you are-- you-- you-- you-- you-- you just did it because you were told. You know, in my 18, 19-year-old mind, didn't even think of protesting it. Well, it wouldn't have done any good.

So one day, the order came that all of us pack one suitcase, and we are going to go to a work-- a work camp. Families will stay together. But there were no men except little boys and old men or the sick.

And so there was a-- an abandoned tobacco factory in the outskirts of town. And we were taken there with our little suitcase. And we had to take some provisions for a few days.

So we lived in this place, you know, like in-- in one room, I don't know, as many as people as they could put-- there were no beds, just on the floor. And my grandmother, bless her soul, she was a-- she had Parkinson's disease. And she was quite young. In those days, I thought she was old, but she was quite young. She was maybe in-- in her late 50s, early 60s.

And-- but she was very arthritic. She couldn't walk very well. So we had to kind of help her up-- we had to have this upstairs, like an attic room. And then from time to time-- we stayed there about four days.

But every day, we had to take our belongings down the stairs. And so the authorities, which were Hungarian soldiers, or they have the gendarmes. They were absolutely vicious, the gendarmes. They were much worse than any police can ever be. And we had to unpack everything and show it to them. And if they found anything to their liking, they took. So we went back.

And there was one incident. It was-- my-- my-- my grandmother was a very religious person. And I had to say prayers all the time-- and very strictly kosher. And we had a-- this piece of sausage. And to this days, 50 years, it has been haunting me that we made her eat that because there was nothing else. [CRYING] [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] It's very difficult. [PAUSES FOR 8 SECONDS]

Anyhow, so the-- after the four or five days that we spent in this place, they said, OK, we're going to go to this work camp. And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] we took one suitcase. And because it was a factory, the railroad tracks went through.

So the cattle cars came, and we were piled in. Oh, but there was something that I had to tell you. While we were still there, they took a-- singled out wealthier people because it was suggested that some of them buried jewelry in the cemetery.

And so there was this one woman, because her husband was in this labor camp. She was taken and beaten to a pulp. And the father of my fiancee was also taken, because they were well-off.

And I don't know if I may be as graphic as that, but he came back totally crazed. They tied a-- a string on his penis and tortured him mercilessly. So he was not a-- a-- a sane person anymore.

So-- but we-- we were packed into these cattle cars, and maybe 45, 50 people and a suitcase, without any facilities. And people were sitting, if they could, on their-- on their suitcases or bundles. And they tried.

And there was a bucket, I presume, someplace, if somebody had to go-- you had to go to the bathroom. And so-- but, you know, when your humanity or humaneness is taken away from you, then I don't think that you-- you feel any shame.

It's-- it's so-- and they still said stay together, stay together. We're going to go to a family work camp. So the-- the journey, this horrible, horrible journey, lasted for four days. And maybe eight to 10 people died during this journey. And when the cattle cars' door opened, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] we found ourselves in the middle of nowhere.

And a man in a-- a striped coat came to-- to help us off. And he says, just leave your belongings where they are. You're going to get them later, maybe. Some of them said, you're going to be dead in five minutes. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

Couldn't imagine where in the world are we-- the railroad tracks and the lines and hundreds and thousands of people. And in the farthest, they sent-- and because my grandmother couldn't walk very well, two of these striped coat men who has-- who have been inmates of Auschwitz for some time by then, they took her. They led her away with them.

So we had to stand in-- in like a formation, four abreast, men separate and women separate. So we were walking ahead. And in the distance, there was this gate, which is by now infamous, arbeit macht frei, work makes you free. And so-- and barbed wires everywhere, and so it was a very, very strange. It didn't look-- look to us like any work place. [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS]

So finally we-- we were-- my aunt and-- I had this cousin also, with-- whom I mentioned to you before, this [? Lily, ?] with us. So there were the three of us. And when our-- our turn came to be the end-- the head of the line, there was this handsome man in shiny boots, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] pointing to the left, pointing to the right. And there, we saw my grandmother, sitting in a ditch. [PAUSES FOR 8 SECONDS]

My aunt got down on her knees to beg him to let us go with her, with my grandma. [CRYING] But he just pointed us to the other side. If he did allow it, I wouldn't be here to tell you this story. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Because she was probably dead within minutes. And I don't know. I hope she didn't suffer too much. [CRYING]

From that point on, we were-- we had to march. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] So in-- in front of some of these barbed wire barracks and herded into a room. They said it's the shower. And of course, there were no belongings, nothing, just the clothes on our backs.

They said, please undress, and take a shower. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] So [SIGHS] later on, we found out that the showers sometimes didn't work as a shower, but the gas could have come out of there too.

We-- OK. So there was this-- this room. And there were young women and men in uniforms-- the women were not in uniforms. This turned out to be Jewish girls who were-- who had been there, I think from Czechoslovakia, because the Czech girls were taken way before we-- we were taken.

And they shaved our heads completely bald, like the top of my hand. And they were cracking jokes. And here we are, totally naked.

We had to bend over. And they pumped some kind of disinfectant into every body cavity that you can think of. And it was awful, because you couldn't hide your nakedness. It was really awful. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

Then we took a shower. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] And there was-- when we got out of the shower, there was a table full of clothing. And so we go and take a clothes, take a shoe. I didn't get to have any kind of a clothing. So the first month of my stay in Auschwitz, I was wrapped in a horse blanket.

And we didn't recognize each other. And the reason I think that my-- Irene must've known something was happening, I had long black hair. And she says, honey, you don't need that long hair. Why don't we cut some of it? So because she thought that if I don't have these long, beautiful hair, then I could save it.

Anyhow, We were then again herded into this huge room. The pictures that you see from Auschwitz with the barracks, you know, there is a famous picture where Elie Wiesel is on also. People are lying in two, three decks, a-- a-- a bunk bed, so to say. But that this was not it. Because the-- the area where I stayed was Birkenau, where the gas chambers and the-- the-- the ovens were.

And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] we went to this large room, and they told us, OK. Sit down tailor fashion. So you know, close, like sardines. And so we sat, and we sat, and we sat, and nothing happened.

So when the night came, we were dead tired. So, you know, you lie down on top of each other. These barracks had a couple of windows and a-- a little room attached on one end, where two girls, the-- in German, they called them Aufseherins. These are the people who were overseers. Overseer is the translation.

And they were Jewish girls, but these Jewish girls felt nothing anymore. They said that oh, you were still going to the theater when we were rotting here already. And so they were not really very pleasant. Later, one was.

Anyhow, so if you had to go to the bathroom, it was really tough luck, because there was no bathroom. So outside, if you-- you know, you had to walk through, I don't know, hundreds of bodies to reach the door, or maybe a-- a few steps less if you wanted to go out the window.

And there were buckets. You know, there were four buckets with a-- a-- a plank on top of it and a latrine sort of thing. And OK, so you go to the bathroom. And when you come-- came back, you didn't find your little bitty spot on the floor, because it was all taken.

The next morning, the stars were still up. Had to-- OK, the whistle blows. Up, everybody out. And they were counting, as they were counting us constantly. Had to stay five-- five in a line, in a row.

And then it was breakfast time. Breakfast consisted of some kind of a brown liquid. God only knows what it was. And the first person in line got a container. And you take a few swallow. You pass it back and pass it back. And the last, the fifth person was hoping if he-- if she is lucky, then she would get a few swallows also. So this-- this was breakfast.

Lunch, I-- I hate to think what was in that. It was some kind of a slop. Unfortunately, there was some threads of meat in it. What it was, I don't even want to think about. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] But it was the same system. There were no utensils. You take a few swallows, pass it back.

And dinner was a piece of bread, brown bread, and we had-- the Germans had this very, very ripe cheese. This was-- there were-- it was a round cheese, and it was so ripe that it-- it could have walked in, practically. And so that was a-- that-- and nothing to drink or anything. There was no water there at all.

Once in a blue moon, they brought the water from a-- in a-- like a tank car or something. And they-- they dumped it into a-- like a trough. And so you know, if you were lucky, you could get, with your hands, water.

This was going on for a long time. And that was little thing. Every morning, get up early, crack of dawn, being counted. Then they started to take some of-- some people away. Where they took them, we didn't know. Never came back. So you know, now I know that they were taken to the gas chamber. They couldn't kill us off all at once.

So just to-- so every day, when we were staying still together, the three of us, we were very happy. Happy. And we spent the day talking about food, food. You would be surprised how much you can talk about food.

We made up menus, elaborate menus. None of us had parties like that, that we were planning, and constantly the food. We were very hungry.

Mothers and daughters and sisters, they fought like cats and dogs for a piece of bread. It is unbelievable what can happen to a human being. It is absolutely unbelievable. My Irene was suffering from hunger more than anybody else. So what I did many times, I sold my cheese for a piece of bread, so then she could have an extra piece of bread. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

It was a scarlet fever epidemic that happened in that camp. People were dying like flies. Trucks came into the camp, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] and the dead bodies were taken out of the barracks, piled up, and it was a two-man job. One person had the arm, one person had the leg, and alley-oop up to the truck. And you know what? We watched it. That-- I don't know. That was-- that was the thing to see in the evening. [PAUSES FOR 6 SECONDS]

My aunt, unfortunately, became also very sick. And I was petrified, petrified that I will lose her, that either she will die, or while she is sick, they take me away. And there was a-- a place where they kind of separated the sick ones, in the same barrack where we were. And I went to lie down among them. I wanted to get sick also, so whatever happens to her should happen to me.

But then one day, for some unknown reason, they decided that they are going to open up a barrack just for the sick ones, you know, like a hospital of sorts. Not with any-- any assistance, but they should be separated. You know, this also makes absolutely no sense.

So I went, because they asked-- they needed help to stuff a-- straw mattresses for these people. So it was like, oh, maybe 200 yards from where we were. And so finally, she was-- I know she was laying down on a-- on-- on a mattress instead of the floor.

And we had to go-- some people were chosen, four or five people, to go to another camp, into Auschwitz, to pick up bucket of food. And so at one time I was one of these people. And a-- a group of men were marching towards us. And all of a sudden, one of them threw a little, tiny green apple to me.

And it was a man from my hometown. I don't remember his name. God bless him. And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] so I had this treasure. I had an apple.

And I remember sinking my teeth into that apple. And I was begging a guard who was guarding that hospital to let me go to the-- to my aunt, to give her the apple. And he did. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] So she had the apple. I hope that cured her, because she got better.

But while she was away for a-- a couple of weeks in this place, I was hiding. And there was one Czech girl who let me jump Irene out the window when they came to take people away. Because I didn't want to leave her. And so that's how-- how-- how we survived.

At one time, on these-- these-- on Sunday, Sunday, breakfast was something sweet, a white, sweet liquid. And like barley water, but they put some maybe sweet-- some kind of saccharine or something. And I loved sweet then, and I love sweet now.

And so I had a swallow. And then I noticed that the row next to me had only four people. So I went over there to stand to be the fifth, so I could have another swallow of that stuff. And this girl saw it, and I had to be punished.

So my-- my punishment was that in front of this overseer's window, I had to kneel on bricks, holding two-- a brick on each hand, holding up these brick. If by the sheer weight, my arm would lower, I had a beating on my shoulders. So I don't know how long did I-- did I have to do that, but it seemed like eternity. That was one punishment. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

Another time-- once a month, they took us to the shower, to take a shower. And it was absolutely amazing that from the first day on, none of us had a menstrual period, except this one poor girl. And it was absolutely awful to see. You know. She is walking stark naked, and the blood dribbling down her leg.

And anyhow, we went to this shower. So we had to walk through a-- like a forest, because it was quite a distance. The-- the-- the-- the bath was in Auschwitz. I-- it must have been, because it was quite a distance.

And we went through this forest. And-- and you were talking to me earlier about the rays of the sun. Well, the sun shone through the trees. And you know, in that hell, I found beauty in that, [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] The sun rays through the trees.

They were-- we were given a piece of soap. And by then, I knew what the soap is made of. I couldn't use the soap. For almost a year, I hid that piece of soap under my arm. And when I went back, after the war, it was buried in the cemetery. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] Because they made soap out of my people. [CRYING] [DRINKS]

One time, when we were walking back from this bath, I found a little, empty bottle, a little bottle. And I don't know for what, I picked up that bottle. Because I had nothing. I guess I wanted to have something that is mine. I had nothing, nothing, nothing, zilch. And so I picked up the bottle. Here I am in my horse blanket and the bottle.

One of these girls, this Czech Jewish girl, saw the bottle. And I had to stand in front of her. And she started to beat the soup out of me. And on this side of my cheek bone, with that bottle, she was hitting me and hitting me and hitting me.

And I never shed a tear. I did not give her the satisfaction to-- that she sees me cry. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] But I survived that too. And that was just one of many beatings that I got. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

This was going on for three months. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And it was Rosh Hashanah. We knew it was Rosh Hashanah, in September of 1944, when we-- they put us again in the-- in a train. And the train went to Bergen-Belsen. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS]

I must say, I was one of the better camps. Everybody knows about them. It took several days. And it was September, end of September. I stayed in Bergen-Belsen for three months. And it was a-- we were also lying on the floor, but everybody got a blanket.

And so we put two blankets on the floor. And we were five, my-- my cousin, my aunt and me, and then two others. So we had three other blankets that we-- we put on top of us. And then that's the way we slept. And so one had to turn during the night, then everybody had to turn. Otherwise, you lost the blankets.

INT: --Barbara Rona, survivor.

BR: And so we arrived in Bergen-Belsen. And that's the way we spent our time there, nothing, again, counting and counting, counting. And the weeks go by. And I-- I became very sick. I had some-- I don't know what is it, a-- a sickness where you-- it affects your ear.

Anyhow, I had the-- I had a busted eardrum, so just oozing. And they had a-- like an infirmary there. And so I went there. And I got a shot in my tush. And to this day, I have that-- I have a-- a-- not to hole, really, would have an extra hole. But it-- it was a dirty needle, so I got an infection there.

And that was-- they were bunk beds, about-- a small room, four beds, maybe. There was this poor girl, lying on the floor. She was from Budapest. She was dying terribly. And my aunt came to the window to talk to me. And I-- I was very sick. It's very painful when your ear busts.

And-- but I got a piece of bread with jam. And so that was absolutely heaven. But I gave it to her, not even thinking that she could get sick. But she did-- she didn't. So she-- she survived. And so anyhow, that's-- we spent three-- three months there.

And the weather turned bitter cold, bitter, bitter cold. And Bergen-Belsen is in northern part of Germany, so winter came early. And I had no underwear. I had one of these-- no socks or hose or nothing. Had shoes and a-- and some kind of, I think, the striped number.

And for some-- magically, a transport came from Budapest, some Jewish people. And these were families. And lo and behold, we found a cousin who was a first cousin to Irene. And she had all her stuff. She came in a fur coat, mind you, to Bergen-Belsen. It was ridiculous. [LAUGHS] She didn't have it for long.

But she gave me a pair of underwear. And you know, I had that underwear for years and years and years. And I wanted to find it, and I couldn't find it. I don't know what happened to it. Anyhow, I had underwear, and it was wonderful. But we were not together, so we couldn't stay together with her.

But then in-- in January of 1945, they were-- we were taken again to another place. And we didn't know where. So-- but we arrived to a-- a-- the-- the concentration camp was Buchenwald, but it was-- the outside of Buchenwald, there was a-- an airplane factory. And they had facilities for-- for workers, so that's where we were put. And we were working in this-- it's called Aschersleben, was the little town where this factory was.

And these three months, actually, were spent semi-human circumstances. We were given a little better food, and my hair started to grow. And as anywhere else, I guess, love bloomed. I-- I met this young man who was a-- a Belgian inmate. They have been there for some time. And we were not allowed to talk.

But if there is a will, there is a way. [LAUGHS] And so he wrote me the most beautiful love letters. And I still have them. I gave them to my son. And there was an older Belgian man who, on the work bench, was drawing maps and trying to explain to me that the Americans are here, the Russians are here. It cannot last much longer. [SIGHS]

But unfortunately for them, it lasted long enough. And I have a [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] ring that this young man-- his name was [? Jean-Pierre. ?] I don't remember his last name. I tried to find him after the war. This-- he made this ring for me out of a coin. It has a little heart on it. I don't know if you can see it-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] tiny little ring. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] And another gift was this heart-shaped little broken button. These were treasures. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] And one day, they were taken, and I never saw them again.

So we stayed at this place where we had to weld broken, rusty pieces of aircraft, good-- absolutely good for nothing, especially by then. It was March of '44. It was totally hopeless.

And then again, they decided to-- that we have to march on. You know? What for? I don't know. What for, I don't know. Maybe they wanted to save their skin, the Nazis, or these SS people.

So we went on a-- on a march. March, march, march, walking and walking and walking, and day and night. And people who couldn't walk were shot right then and there, at the side of the road.

Irene was saying, I cannot go on. I cannot go on. And I says, yes, you can. Yes, you can. We are going to make this if it kills us-- no pun intended. The--

INT: This was 1945?

BR: 1940-- '45, spring 1945. And the rain came down, and it rained, and it rained, and it rained. And we were wet and no place to-- to-- in-- inside to-- to stay. Once in a blue moon, a farmer allowed us to sleep in the-- in the barn. But in the morning came, we still had to go on in the same wet clothes.

And then at one-- one time, the Americans, I imagine it, they were, they were flying overhead, and we were just marching. And then they-- they were strafe-- the planes were shooting down, because we practically could touch them. And the-- the villages were burning, and we just had to march and march. And don't look at anything, just march.

And some of us had to pull the wagon like oxen, the-- the-- the Nazis' belongings. At that point, I think that they already tore off the insignia, but they were, of course, in uniform. But they knew that the end-- end is coming. But they still had marchers, and they were still shooting.

Then at one-- we were marching towards Czechoslovakia, which I didn't know at the time where we were going, we just go. And then we put on the-- the-- the last leg of this trip was put in a-- a-- on a train. And-- and again, I was sick. I don't know, you know, because of all this wetness and the-- and the wetness.

Oh, I didn't tell you that was-- that it affected the rest of my life, that I told myself when I saw that rain. And there was some houses in the distance where the lights were on. And I thought to myself, gee, those lucky son of a guns. They are having a-- a nice house, warm house, and they are inside. And here I am, outside in the rain.

And I-- if I survive this, the rain is going to just absolutely-- if I can watch the rain from my warm house, that will be a-- a biggest gift in my life. And I am never complained. It can rain, it can rain as much, by buckets. I just love the rain.

The-- so we-- they put us finally on a train. And I was sick. And there was a-- the Nazis, the guards, were in a separate compartment. And I was lying on the floor. I had very high fever. And, you know, one Nazi came out as a soldier, and he called me into his little compartment.

I really wasn't afraid of him at that time, because what can he do to me anymore? [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] You know what he did? He gave me a half a lemon. That was absolutely incredible.

That was one human kindness that I found from a German. And also during that march, some of the civilians who were standing in line for food, and as this one man passed us by, he put a-- a can of food into either my aunt or my cousin's hand. And it was a can of lard.

I don't remember how we opened that can, but we did. And we ate that lard with our hands. How we didn't get sick, I don't know. But that was one kindness. Because that man, whoever he was, I hope he found his reward somewhere. Because he-- he showed that in-- in that hell, it-- it can be-- somebody can have a-- human feelings.

When this train trip was over, we found ourselves in a field. In the distance, they have-- we saw a city of sort, a town. And so it turned out to be Theresienstadt. The Czech name for it is Terezín.

And there were girls or women working on the fields. And we went to a-- a-- it looked-- on the outside, it looked like a regular town with apartments, but they really were not. It was a-- a-- Theresienstadt was one of those camps that was made for Red Cross purposes.

It was-- they had sometimes a-- a visit, visitations from the Red Cross, and then they-- it looked like there's a beauty shop, and there is a bakery, and there-- the-- the people-- the people were printed, you know, like Monopoly money and they buy something, but there was nothing. So there was like a facade.

And we were full of-- had lice, you know, not only head lice, but body lice. And-- and-- and the lice spreads disease, of course. And there was a-- nothing to eat. Food was very, very sparse.

And somehow somebody brought us potato peelings. And you know, potato peels taste mighty good if you are hungry. I just read in the JewishJournalthe other day that these people, also survivors, they put a potato peel on the Seder plate. So I thought to myself, I'm going to do that too this year.

Potato peel is wonderful. And if you were lucky enough to have a friend who was working in the kitchen, then you could get an extra dose of potato peel, or maybe even a raw potato or a half-rotten turnip or something. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

The-- the war, we didn't know what's going on, but it was just a-- a-- a place. It-- it was-- it was pretty terrible, but not nearly as-- as the caliber of Auschwitz or Bergen-Belsen.

Finally, the eighth of May, the Russians, the first Russian soldier came, on a-- on a-- on a horse, I think. And these Jews who were half-dead, they lifted up that soldier and carried him into the camp on their-- on their shoulders.

And it turned out to be they were not very lovely people, because the officers-- or soldiers, regular foot soldiers, they were pulling out these skeletal women that we were-- I was 90 pounds-- of the-- of the restroom, you know, for entertainment. And a-- a-- an officer came with a gun to try to make order there.

But that's-- that's the way. And so they-- they-- they-- they didn't even know, well, who are these people? They never saw anything like that. And so they were not-- they were not the enemy. They were not-- they were not soldiers. They were-- who are these?

And so it took a while. It took a long while until-- until, you know, they-- they could provide us with some kind of food. And I don't know if there was medication, because I was, again, sick as a dog from the typhoid fever, from the lice. And there was a doctor from our hometown who have had absolutely nothing, we know. But how, where he got two-- he gave me two aspirins. And I-- I recovered.

And that was May 8th. And the end of June, there was a transport that we could get to Hungary. I wanted to see my dad. And I didn't know that in 1944, October 13, the war getting-- was very close to the place where he was attached to this Hungarian unit in-- in Hungary.

And he and 70 other of his comrades hid. And a-- a-- a woman saw them and told it to the authorities. And they found them. They had to dig a hole. And all 70 of them were shot into this common grave.

Now, two days later, the war was over, because the Russians occupied that town. And my father's friend, you know, from our town, was on the unit that exhumed this grave. And I-- I have a copy of that official document, where they identified the bodies. And [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] my father had a thigh wound, [CRYING] So he was buried alive. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] It's unbearable. [CRYING]

I have a-- some dirt that I brought out to the United States from that grave. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] [SIGHS] We went on a transport, this time on flat cars to-- towards Budapest, from Czechoslovakia. We arrived in the-- this railroad station, outskirts of Budapest. And there was a-- a train official who hollered over to us, oh, you came back! I thought you were all dead. That was the welcoming committee. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

Then in Budapest, we found out that there is an organization that try, where people write down their names, whoever comes back, and try to find a-- family or friends or relations. I went there. And I-- I-- I-- that's the time I found out about my father.

And I remember running out of that place like a bat out of hell, screaming, there is no God, there is no God. Here I am, liberated, for what? I have nothing. I have nobody. I have no home. I have no family. I have no house. I have nothing. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS]

And [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] then we-- after a few days of that, my-- Irene and my cousin and I went to Békéscsaba. And for some reason, my husband-to-be's parents were there. And they took me in with open arms.

And [SIGHS] that was in end of June, early July. And well, I had nothing, you know. I have nothing of-- of my belongings at all. [DRINKS] Then the-- somehow life started to fall into place. I don't even remember what I did-- nothing much.

And we were just waiting, who is coming, who is coming home. And then one day, the-- the word came that Lou Rona, who became my husband, is in the next town. And so my brother-in-law went on a bicycle and go get him. And so he came home.

I was nine years old when I met him for the first time. And then these-- these common horrendous things brought us together. And I married him, and I loved him with all of my heart. And unfortunately, he was taken away from me at a very early age. He was only 58 years old, 18 years ago. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS]

But he gave me [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] three children, [CRYING] the reason for my existence. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] And he couldn't have left me with a more precious legacy. [PAUSES FOR 7 SECONDS]

I have four grandchildren. The oldest is 18 years old, my first number one munchkin. She's at Reed College in Oregon. And she is coming home. I think she's going to be here pretty soon. And [? Jessica, ?] her sister, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] and then I have [? Daniel. ?] These are all my son Bob's children.

And my daughter, Shoshana, [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] and my youngest, Jeff and his-- and his wife, [? Judy, ?] and they have a little [? Isaac. ?] [? Isaac ?] happens to be a girl, even though her name is [? Isaac. ?] Not the biblical [? Isaac-- ?] she was named after [? Isaac ?] [? Denison, ?] the author that's really [INAUDIBLE]. She's a cutie. She just turned two years old.

So those four children are the dividends of my life. Give me an extreme amount of joy. I don't know if you want at this point to look at some of these photographs? Not yet?

And I have-- in the United States-- oh, which is a very important thing. My oldest son was born in-- in Germany. Because of all places, after the-- after the war was over, and my mother-in-law had two sisters here who sent us papers and work affidavits that we will not be burdens of the state, and we should just come. And we heard that there are camps in Germany, and we just come. And in a week, two months, we can come to the United States.

So we did that. Snuck through the border on a covered truck, was supposed to have been some kind of cargo. And the-- the leader of this, who organized these trips, a young Jewish fella, he gave a bottle of vodka to the border guard, you see? Right shortly after, within the first few months of the end of the war, it wasn't quite as strict as later it became, where they had all these guard towers.

And so we arrived in Vienna, where we spent a few days. And then we were dispersed and on-- on the train, and again left in the middle of nowhere in-- in Germany. So I don't know if I can describe to you the feeling when after spending that hell in Germany, here I am, back in Germany again. It is just unbelievable.

We-- we arrived-- the town's name was Ulm, in the heart of Southern Germany. And we spent in a-- in a-- a-- in a camp. It was a displaced person camp. In a-- and-- and it was a-- it used-- used to belong to soldiers. Here I was, newly married, and we were 16 people in the room. That's how I spent my honeymoon, really, with 16 other people.

And then we worked there for a while. I didn't. After a while, I-- I-- I found some work. And my husband was working in the-- you know, in the infirmary. And it was-- so believe it or not, it took almost five years, five years, precious five years taken from my life, or our lives, to wait to come into the United States in a displaced person camp.

So when we came to the United States, finally, you know, I-- I really didn't want to have a baby, but he-- he just happened. And so he was 10 months old when we came. He was born in Ulm, the same place where Albert Einstein was born, so that claim to fame. [LAUGHS] They don't-- didn't name a street after him. They didn't name one after Einstein either. There was a little alley that was named after him. After all, he's a Jew.

So we came to the United States. We arrived January the 20th after a hard and horrendous journey on the ship, on the Atlantic Ocean, on this Navy transport boat.

INT: January 20th?

BR: January 20, yes.

INT: Of what year?

BR: Of 1940-- no, no, no. I'm sorry. 1951. And coming from that ice cold, we-- we boarded the ship in Bremen, in-- in Germany. And it was ice cold. And we came here, and it was one of those Los Angeles heat waves. So it was a-- quite an adjustment to make, needless to say.

You know, my husband was one of those old-fashioned guys that, you know, women are supposed to take care of babies and not go to work. So I didn't go to work. But we somehow managed. He went to school and took all kinds of classes. And he started to work for a-- a cosmetic company. And eventually he became a senior chemist at Max Factor, where he spent the last 20 years of his life.

And when my youngest was in school, I started to work. And I also took classes in early childhood education, started to work with children. And I went to work in a-- first in a nursery school, and then in a regular school. And I-- because I was home when my children came home, and that was important for all of us.

And for many, many years, many years, we never talked about this. You know, when the wound is so deep, and it is so painful, and it's so hurting that you just cannot constantly open it up. And besides, we wanted to raise children who are not burdened with the package that their parents had to carry.

And so I hope we managed this. You will see them shortly. And so unfortunately, my husband couldn't see the dividends, because he died a few months, and I was-- my daughter-in-law was pregnant already, so he knew that there is a munchkin coming. But-- [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] but I think that's about it.

INT: What was your marriage date?

BR: January 10, the birthday of my mother, 1946. In one more year, we would have been married for 50 years.

INT: When you look back, what was your motivating force for survival?

BR: For survival? I did not have any motivating force. It was just coincidence. I don't believe it had anything to do with strength or-- or willpower, nothing. Because I was in circumstances where I really didn't give a hoot, you know, being a refined person as I am. If I die or live or not, what-- no, no. You don't-- you-- you lose all fear. You really do.

INT: You mentioned at one point that you hid out of a window.

BR: Yes.

INT: I'd like to just go back. Where did you hide, and what did-- how long--

BR: Oh, just from one barrack to the other. So-- because I wanted to stay and-- and hid behind the door. but you know, just-- so maybe there was some kind of a power above me that protected me so they didn't take me any of those transports. So I could-- I could wait until we can leave together with my aunt.

INT: And what about your loving Aunt Irene?

BR: Yeah, my-- my sweet Irene came out here also and married a-- her love of-- of-- of her life after my father died. And he was Tibor Janke, who was a-- an artist. He died a year ago, at the age of 94. And he-- he-- he was also-- he-- he-- he would have had a story also to tell.

But we all did. I always used to say that everybody, all survivors, our stories are like a jigsaw, part of a jigsaw puzzle that you would put together. See, my husband could tell a story of his own. I wish he could. And what was the question?

INT: Your-- what happened to your Aunt Irene?

BR: Oh, Irene died six months prior to my husband. He died in '76. She-- she died October 1975. She had a stroke, and she died. And she left me too.

INT: How did your relationship continue after the liberation?

BR: Absolutely the most fantastic. Was most fantastic person. And I loved her, and I still love her. And I still feel her presence with me.

INT: Your Cousin [? Lily ?] also?

BR: Yeah. She also survived, and she is back in-- in Hungary. She is a grandmother by now. So-- and occasionally we-- we were in touch, but you know, sometimes out of sight, out of mind. So I haven't seen her since.

I never could make myself go back to Hungary. Went to Europe a few times from here, but never, ever to Hungary. Sometimes I feel that I would like to go to Auschwitz, if maybe I could get healed, or it would be maybe a catharsis.

I don't know. I don't know. I would like to touch the ground, full of bones. [PAUSES FOR 5 SECONDS] But then I guess I just don't have the guts.
INT: --the survivor. [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] Did you have something else that you wanted to share?

BR: Yes. I wanted to tell you-- which I forgot. At-- at the very first few days when we arrived in Auschwitz, first of all, because we were-- they didn't bother anymore to-- to tattoo numbers on us anymore. It-- it was-- it was strictly-- I don't know if there is an English-- yes. And-- and it was a Vernichtungslager, which means annihilation, you know? That was the-- they didn't bother anymore with numbers.

But at the same time, how-- how the mentality works, I recall having a-- a long table set up. And there were picture postcards, beautiful picture postcards. And we each had to write to-- to a relative back home. Of course it were never-- never sent. But maybe they were.

Anyhow, I sent it to my father. And of course, my father was dead by then. But it-- it was a picture postcard of a resort place called [? Waldensee. ?] Now, I don't know if there is such a place or not, probably. But we all also-- we had to-- we arrived, and we are having a good time in [? Waldensee, ?] love so and so. Can you believe that? Isn't that the most incongruous thing that you ever heard?

It's-- it-- it boggles the mind. See? The-- it's a cover-up maybe. I don't know what was the reason. But that's what they had to do.

INT: Did that have any effect on your writing postcards today?

BR: Well, it will-- I don't write. I-- I call on the telephone. [LAUGHS] I don't write letters. I-- I'm a terrible letter writer.

INT: When you and your husband Louis came to America, what experiences or pressures did you feel when you arrived?

BR: Well, I'll tell you. My first impression, we went to-- you see, Irene and Tibor, her husband, they came earlier than we did. And they lived on Vermont Avenue, in a two-bedroom apartment. And so we stayed with them for a few weeks, until we could find something of our own.

And we were looking for an apartment to rent. Now, that was 1951, January, February, March or so. And because-- you know, I could-- I learned-- during the-- in school, I learned English. So I had school English, but I was deathly afraid to talk to people.

And we asked a-- a-- a-- a cousin of mine, [? Eleanor, ?] to-- to answer these ads for looking for an apartment. And do you know, the first person, it was apartment for rent, one-bedroom apartment. And they ask if we were Jews.

And I says, my god almighty. I am here, after what I went through, and I come to the free United States of America, and they-- they-- the-- the landlady ask if we are Jewish? And I said, that's terrible.

So I don't really recall any other personal antisemitic statements, but sometimes, you never knew if you have little nuances, that they ask questions, or you feel-- but I am-- I am a very happy and very proud citizen of the United States. This is the first home I ever had and that I really feel I belong. And I-- I would defend this country against all enemies.

INT: Have people asked you about your experiences through the years?

BR: Oh, yes, yes. And I-- I couldn't talk about it for many years. But in the-- the last several years, I have been talking to children, lots of children. Not too long ago, from the 1st of February, actually, I also have a videotape of that, my daughter's school.

And these are all Latino children, mind you. And she and another teacher took them. And these are advanced, you know, gifted kids, like maybe there were 25, 30 kids.

They took them to the Museum of Tolerance first, and then they invited me, and another man who was from Budapest, and a friend of mine who is a Polish girl who was hidden and survived by the-- the grace and-- and the bravery of a Polish woman and as a German maid.

Anyhow, so the three of us talk. But then we-- any time, every time that I'm asked, I talk about it freely. Of course I gear it to children. Even though children love to listen to gory stories, I am not going into any details.

And the last time when I spoke at Shoshana's school, it was difficult for me to-- to speak in front of her. And also Bob, my oldest, came to listen. They wanted to see their mom in action. But you know, it's-- I-- I can't stand the idea of hurting them by-- you know, with what I went through.

INT: By what you went through, do you feel you raised your children in any special way from these events?

BR: [SIGHS] I really don't think so. I think I just raised them the best way I knew how. And they are all-- my oldest is a president of his temple.

And so it's-- you know, they are-- they are good, solid citizens. Of course they know. I'm-- I'm sure that they have talked about it among themselves. But we didn't make any emphasis of it. We never wanted to put a-- a burden on them that, look, you are having it so good. And when we were your age, look what we had to go through. Never-- wouldn't even enter our minds.

INT: Have you made any decisions in your life or based anything on these experiences?

BR: Yes, to live every day to the fullest. And-- and-- and but you know what? That's easier said than done. Because I still worry about tomorrow, and I don't know if tomorrow ever comes. But it is a-- that's-- that's-- that's the lesson that we all should learn, really, live it to the fullest.

When I was growing up-- and that's-- that's one thing I was taught. That was an effect on-- on my life on theirs. My father used to tell me, you just learn all that you can, because that's the only thing. Knowledge is the only thing that nobody can take away from you.

And so I used to tell them to-- you just learn-- when-- even if I needed help, they had-- said the magic word, homework, OK, you go. I'll do whatever, you know, doing dishes or chores. Nobody had to do anything if they said the magic word, homework. [LAUGHS]

INT: Have your feelings about religion in general changed?

BR: I am not sure if I would have a-- if I didn't have any children, I don't know. Of course, you know, that-- that would have been a different person. I don't know if I would have been religious at all. Because I don't know if-- you talk about God, so where was God? You know?

It is a-- you-- you read about Jewish history, you read the Torah. And it's every-- every Monday and Thursday, there was another killings, you know, in our history, in our past. And so if-- I thought to myself, if this is a kind and loving, forgiving god, how can he do that? What in the world did we do? If we are the chosen people, he chose us certainly for the wrong reasons.

But-- but then the children came. And I felt it's very important for the children to get a religious education, because they cannot just grow up like a weed. And so I became involved. So I belong to this little temple here in Mar Vista, and I go Friday night. I go every Saturday. I-- and-- and I enjoy it. And I enjoy it.

But when it was in January, this past January, the 50th anniversary of the liberation of Auschwitz, it happened to be a Friday night, and I couldn't utter a sound. I couldn't-- I couldn't say a prayer. [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] So there-- there is a great deal of deep underlying effects.

INT: You graciously consented to be part of this visual history. Why must it be remembered?

BR: Why? [PAUSES FOR 4 SECONDS] Well, it-- the answer is very obvious. Hopefully, hopefully enough people will see and hear these stories and realize that only our-- you know, my people, 6 million Jews, were killed for no other reason than they were-- happened to be born Jewish. You know? I could have been born into a-- the Smith family, and then I-- I-- I wouldn't have had to go through this-- this horror.

So if there is one thing that they should learn, hopefully someday people will learn to live in peace and leave each other alone and judge a person by the individual and not put everybody under one umbrella, that all the Jews are no good, and all these are no good, and that people are no good. You can-- next time, you could single out that all blue eyed people can go, and they-- they cannot enter-- enter the-- the-- the home of neighborhood of their choice. So you mustn't. You mustn't have a-- a generalization. That's what I'm trying to say.

INT: Is there anything else that you would like to add, any experiences, any thoughts, any feelings that we didn't ask today?

BR: Well, you know, actually, if I would go through like a diary, that would be a-- it would be a-- a tremendously long tape. And because the-- man's inhumanity to man, it is so indescribable. And-- and-- and, you know, incidents like they were-- they were-- there was some clothing at one point delivered. I don't even remember if it was Bergen-Belsen, I think it was.

And we wanted to have something warm to wear. And-- and we went, and I don't know why, they started to beat people, like on a-- with a two by four. And both Irene and I got such whacks on our backs with these two by fours, poor thing, she was suffering all her life from those-- those beating.

And, you know, these things were going on and on and on. And after a while, you became immune to it. You know? So it's hard to believe, but you do. And at one time, you know, there was a-- a-- yes, I think it's EJ-- EG Farben, a-- a factory outside of Auschwitz, very, very close. And it was bombed at one point by the Americans.

And, you know, we were hoping that they are going to bomb us to smithereens in there, but they didn't. I don't know why. But they should have at least bombed the railroads. But pieces were falling from here and there, and we were absolutely disregarding it. So you lose all fear of death. Your-- your-- your life means nothing, nothing.

INT: OK. We are going to meet some of your family in a few moments. This tape is also going to be preserved for your family.

BR: I am thrilled about that.

INT: What would you like to say directly to your family right now?

BR: First of all, I would like-- I want to-- I want them to know, which they know already, that it is such a privilege for me not just to have them, but to know them. Individually, they are such super human beings that all parents should be blessed with children like I have and that they should all just continue with the work that they do, and they should love each other, and they should love people. And I don't have to teach them at this time, because they are in their 40s or almost 40, how to behave to other people, because they have learned that through the years.

Because both my father-- my father. My husband was the most ethical, decent person. And so they-- they learned from him work ethic, the love of family, and honor, and so that's the way they are. And I don't need to teach them anything anymore that they don't know.

INT: Barbara, we want to thank you before we go to meeting your family and showing your photographs. And we hope the sweetness that you still like will be there in your life forever.

BR: I sure hope so. And thank you. And I-- I feel very privileged to be chosen for this interview. And I-- I hope that somebody will learn something from it.

INT: Barbara, will you introduce your family to us?

BR: I would be honored and happy to. This is my number one son, Bob.

ROBERT RONA: First born, anyway.

BR: First born anyway. Yeah, right. [LAUGHS] Yeah. And his wife, someplace. Raise your hand, [? Rebecca. ?] And where is [? Allison? ?] [? Allison ?] is my number one munchkin. And her sister, [? Jessica, ?] and [? Daniel, ?] they all belong together. So that's Bob and [? Rebecca ?] and [? Jessica ?] and-- what's your name, [? Allison ?] and [? Daniel. ?] [LAUGHS]

And then comes Shoshana. Shoshana is my daughter, my middle child. And her very special friend, [? Beverly ?] [? Moore, ?] and she's an integral part of our family. And then comes Jeff, and my baby. He's always been my baby. And-- and his wife, [? Judy, ?] and [? Isaac, ?] the little one. You have a birthday party? Huh? Yes?

INT: Bob, could we start with you? Would you like just to add something or what you know of what Mom has gone through, and Dad, your views?

ROBERT RONA: Well, I remember, just thinking about back when I was in-- in school, knowing that even though most of my friends were Jewish, I was the only one of-- of the-- the people that I associated with in school who was the child of survivors. All my friends were-- who-- their parents were also Native American born.

And so I knew that I was different. There was something different about me in-- in that respect. And I think that, you know, growing up knowing that, that there was something different about my background, even compared to those of my close friends, made me more conscious of-- of all that went on.

And even though my parents, unlike some other-- other parents of-- who-- who-- who survived the-- the Holocaust, my parents really didn't talk a whole lot about it in our presence. We knew that something happened, but it wasn't drummed into us day to day.

And I-- I-- I don't know, I sort of have mixed feelings about that. Because maybe I don't know as much about what happened, but I think on the other hand, my parents were trying to spare us some of the agony and maybe some of the guilt that goes along with that. But it's-- it's made me a lot more aware that I am-- there's something different about me and my background. And I'm a lot more aware of inhumanity and the need to improve relations between people. And I think I am more than other people might be as a result of that.

INT: Thank you. Shoshana?

SHOSHANA RONA: Something Bob said triggered a thought for me. He said something about guilt. And being the-- the daughter of survivors, two things really came into my mind. One is that as an adolescent especially, when I began learning really what did happen, not just to my parents, but to all the Jews, I felt terrible guilt that I wasn't there to protect them, which is completely irrational. I wasn't even born till after that.

But the anger and the feeling of why couldn't I have done something was a huge part of my adolescence. I had nightmares about Nazis for years and years and years, which I never really shared, because it was very isolating for me.

And then as I got through that and-- and grew into my own adulthood, I realized something about my-- my mom, that the Holocaust, in a way, gave her and gave then the rest of us a gift, and that is that unlike other people, who became somehow bitter or-- or hostile toward the world, and why did this happen to me, Mom came out of it with, for some reason which I still don't understand, this enormous capacity for loving and for including everybody into her life, that there isn't a sense of, well, there's only a finite amount of love, and we have to keep it in the family. But everybody is always welcome.

And when I was a kid, I could bring home friends, and there was always, you know, a-- another plate at the table and room for another chair and-- and this feeling that all of us, you know, with my brothers and then as-- as we, you know, married and so forth and-- and had our own children, that there was always room for more love. And everybody was welcome and included in the extended family and that friends became like family.

And that's a wonderful thing for my mother's heart to mine, that I try to keep that. That's a-- that's the most precious legacy, I think, that she could have given me, was this tremendous capacity to be open and loving and accepting of-- of everybody. And-- and that's a gift for which I am eternally grateful, because it gives me something that I could never have gotten, I think, if she hadn't had that experience. And so I-- I consider myself blessed to be my mother's daughter, in many ways, and that-- that one perhaps most profoundly was giving me [PAUSES FOR 3 SECONDS] a precious gift that I can carry on to subsequent generations.

INT: Thank you. Jeff, would you like to add something from your feelings?

JEFFREY RONA: I think it might have been a little different for me. I came along later, and so there is that much more distance, seven years between my-- my eldest brother and I. And there's a little more distance. And I didn't really even have an inkling of it for a long time, that there was anything that it meant.

I mean, it was something that was talked about when I was in-- in Sunday school and in other places, but it was never really a topic here. And I didn't really have an awareness of it for quite some time. And then--

[? ISAAC RONA: ?] Mom, I can go down.

JEFFREY RONA: [LAUGHS] Suddenly I can-- I remember when there would-- there would be some flashes of-- of it. Sometimes when there would be some anger, if-- if I were to do something that was selfish, or if any of us were to do something that was selfish or insolent against our parents, some-- every so often, a little flash would come out of you don't know how-- how good you have it, you know. Because when we were-- when we were in the war, this is what happened to us.

But they made it clear that they didn't really want it to be something. They didn't want us to feel sorry for them. And that-- that became something over and over. And then that-- I think that led to some-- some questions.

I think in terms of how it's affected me in the long run, I would say that it-- it has taught me about not counting on things at times. That is, you don't know what's going to stay and what's going to go in your life. So don't-- don't assume anything.

I was instilled with the notion of-- of education, because as my mom would say to me, they can take everything away from you, but they can't take away your knowledge. And I didn't really understand what that meant when I was in college and having a horrible time. But after a while, I think it-- it started to dawn on me that-- that there are things more important than-- than material gain. And-- and those things involve love and responsibility and-- and a sense of-- of learning what's important. And it's not always what's obvious.

INT: Thank you. You have a beautiful family, Barbara.

BR: Thank you.

INT: And we're going to go and share some photographs.

BR: OK.

[? ISAAC RONA: ?] No.

BR: We didn't see those photo--

This is a picture of my parents, Joseph Spinner, and my mother, Roszi [? Alexander. ?] They were on their wedding day, and they got married November 22, 1922.

CREW: Where?

BR: Oh, in Békéscsaba, Hungary.

OK. This is my dad, Joseph Spinner, and myself. And I was about two and a half years old, which must have been around early 1926. And that was a very special relationship between us.

CREW: When was it? Where?

BR: It was also in Békéscsaba. The whole kit and caboodle takes place in Békéscsaba.

This is a picture of my grandmother, who is in the center. She is my maternal grandmother, Ilona [? Alexander. ?] And Irene is on her left, my-- my aunt, Irene, and myself in the light blouse. I was about 16, 17 years old, so it was taken about 1940, '41.

This is our wedding picture. My husband, Lou Rona, and I got married January 10, 1946, in Békéscsaba, Hungary. That was our wedding day.

This is-- I am-- I am here shortly before we left Hungary, after the war. It was taken in May 1946, and in Békéscsaba, Hungary. And I was just a young bride in this picture.

CREW: And your name?

BR: My name is Barbara.

CREW: What Barbara?

BR: Barbara Rona. And by-- I was Barbara Rona by then.

This-- this picture was taken about 22 years ago of myself, me, Barbara, and my husband, Lou Rona, here in Culver City. And I just wanted to have a picture of him also.

This picture shows they vandalized the Jewish cemetery in Békéscsaba, Hungary. And this is the cemetery where my mother and grandfather are buried.

This picture was done after Irene and my description of life in Auschwitz. It was done by my uncle, Tibor Janke. And it depicts the-- the horror of hunger and-- and the few swallows of food that I was earlier talking about.

CREW: It's a drawing, yes?

BR: Yes. It's a charcoal.

